  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p108.s01",
     "source": "Lovers of legendary lore may feast upon this collection of traditional tales of the Hawaiian people and their origin as first told by the old Hawaiians and sometimes touched up and added to by the Hawaiian story-teller."
    },
    {
     "id": "p108.s02",
     "source": "The author was president of the Hawaiian Historical Society for some time, and is a resident of Honolulu."
    },
    {
     "id": "p108.s03",
     "source": "The tales found in this handsomely illustrated volume have already for the most part seen print in papers, magazines, and society reports, and they are well worthy of preservation in this permanent form."
    },
    {
     "id": "p108.s04",
     "source": "The legends tell of many things in heaven and on earth, of the creation of man, the gods who found water, the great dog Ku, the Cannibal Dog-man, the water of life of Kane.— Transcript, Boston, Mass., Aug."
    },
    {
     "id": "p108.s05",
     "source": "11, 1915."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p108"
  },
  {
   "type": "break",
   "segments": [],
   "id": "p109"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p110.s01",
     "source": "\"Legends of Old Honolulu,\" collected and translated by W. D. Westervelt, author of several other fine literary works, is an interesting and fascinating volume in which we are told with beauty of language and colorful description the weird and mysterious folk-lore of these distant people who live in a charmed atmosphere and whose life is one long summer day."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p110"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p111.s01",
     "source": "These legends have been gathered from Hawaiian traditions by W. D. Westervelt, who resides in Honolulu, and who is particularly equipped for giving them to the reading public."
    },
    {
     "id": "p111.s02",
     "source": "They are illustrated with many sepia pictures taken from original photographs, and these add greatly to the charm of the book."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p111"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p112.s01",
     "source": "The author has not lost the simplicity of style in translation, and this makes these tales all the more delightful."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p112"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p113.s01",
     "source": "\"The Great Dog Ku\" is captivating in its unusual depiction."
    },
    {
     "id": "p113.s02",
     "source": "\"The Wonderful Shell\" is a veritable prose poem, and there is magic and wonderful imagery about \"Pikoi the Rat-Killer\" which will enthrall the youngsters and entertain their elders."
    },
    {
     "id": "p113.s03",
     "source": "All these legends have their own particular appeal, and this book may be classed among the rare offerings of the year.— Courier, Buffalo, N. Y., Aug."
    },
    {
     "id": "p113.s04",
     "source": "29, 1915."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p113"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p114.s01",
     "source": "W. D. Westervelt has produced a book of permanent and world-wide interest in collecting and translating the legends of old Honolulu which embody all that the vanishing race knows of their origin and their life before the white man came to civilize and decimate them."
    },
    {
     "id": "p114.s02",
     "source": "The legends are given their proper setting by means of descriptive interludes and explanations of native customs and a key to the language and its pronunciation."
    },
    {
     "id": "p114.s03",
     "source": "No ethnologist, student of comparative religion, or mythologist can afford to be ignorant of the material collected by Mr. Westervelt and embodied in this well printed and finely illustrated little volume."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p114"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p115.s01",
     "source": "Published by Geo."
    },
    {
     "id": "p115.s02",
     "source": "H. Ellis Co., Boston, Mass.— Express, Portland, Me., Sept."
    },
    {
     "id": "p115.s03",
     "source": "4, 1915."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p115"
  },
  {
   "type": "break",
   "segments": [],
   "id": "p116"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p117.s01",
     "source": "Mr. Westervelt has long been an active investigator of the aboriginal conditions of Hawaiian life, and the stories he has discovered have added not a little to our knowledge of the Polynesian race as it was before the dawn of history."
    },
    {
     "id": "p117.s02",
     "source": "The ancient Hawaiians were of an imaginative turn of mind, and their traditions abound in tales of gods and goblins."
    },
    {
     "id": "p117.s03",
     "source": "Some of the stories, now centuries old, are closely related to the legends that are known to exist in New Zealand and other islands of the Pacific, and many of them bear active resemblances to the fairy-tales of our own country."
    },
    {
     "id": "p117.s04",
     "source": "They are interesting enough in themselves, and have an added attraction for the student of comparative folk-lore."
    },
    {
     "id": "p117.s05",
     "source": "The present volume contains excellent illustrations of the scenery of Honolulu, some of them taken from photographs by the author.— Scotsman, Great Britain, Sept."
    },
    {
     "id": "p117.s06",
     "source": "13, 1915."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p117"
  },
  {
   "type": "break",
   "segments": [],
   "id": "p118"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p119.s01",
     "source": "Mr. Westervelt, who gives us these legends of Polynesia, has lived for many years in Honolulu, and has made a special study of the history and traditions of the people of the islands."
    },
    {
     "id": "p119.s02",
     "source": "He writes as one well versed in his subject, and some of the legends which he presents to us are of great beauty, showing a fine and delicate imagination in their authors."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p119"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p120.s01",
     "source": "The character of the legends varies."
    },
    {
     "id": "p120.s02",
     "source": "One or two, and these perhaps the most interesting, are Creation myths."
    },
    {
     "id": "p120.s03",
     "source": "It is evident here and there that the original web is crossed with later strands which have obviously been introduced by Christian missionary teaching, and it is not always easy to disentangle them."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p120"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p121.s01",
     "source": "One, that has as primitive and antique a savour as any, is that of the Hog-god, Kamapuaa."
    },
    {
     "id": "p121.s02",
     "source": "It is a great tale, and Kamapuaa was rather a glorious ruffian and capable of surprising transformations."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p121"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p122.s01",
     "source": "\"Many of the Hawaiians [he writes] of to-day believe in the continual presence of the aumakuas, the spirits of the dead."
    },
    {
     "id": "p122.s02",
     "source": "In time past the aumakuas were a powerful reality."
    },
    {
     "id": "p122.s03",
     "source": "An ancester, a father or a grandfather, a makua, died."
    },
    {
     "id": "p122.s04",
     "source": "Sometimes he went to Po, the under-world, or to Milu, the shadow-land, or to Lani, the Hawaiian heaven, and sometimes he remained to be a torment or a blessing to his past friends.\""
    }
   ],
   "id": "p122"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p123.s01",
     "source": "We could do well with more light thrown on these places, pleasant or unpleasant, and on the ideas of the Polynesians concerning the life after death."
    },
    {
     "id": "p123.s02",
     "source": "It seems that it would be well within Mr. Westervelt's power and knowledge to give us this further light, and we may hope that some day he will do so.— Times, London, Sept."
    },
    {
     "id": "p123.s03",
     "source": "23, 1915."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p123"
  },
  {
   "type": "break",
   "segments": [],
   "id": "p124"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p125.s01",
     "source": "Honolulu is fast becoming a favorite tourist land, and particularly since the tremendous popularity of a recent Hawaiian volcano play, a good many people have taken to humming pensively the native farewell song and discoursing wistfully of the Eden-like qualities of the islands."
    },
    {
     "id": "p125.s02",
     "source": "In view of this increasing interest, W. D. Westervelt's book of the legends of Honolulu is especially timely, although such a work always has value."
    },
    {
     "id": "p125.s03",
     "source": "During his residence in Honolulu this writer has collected and translated from the Hawaiian all the available legends of the region, retelling them with singular success."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p125"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p126.s01",
     "source": "To mention but an instance, every one of them has a tale relating the creation of man."
    },
    {
     "id": "p126.s02",
     "source": "This haunting similarity is one of the fascinations of legend study."
    },
    {
     "id": "p126.s03",
     "source": "Mr. Westervelt has made a noteworthy contribution to that branch of literature.— Bellman, Minneapolis, Minn., Sept."
    },
    {
     "id": "p126.s04",
     "source": "25, 1915."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p126"
  },
  {
   "type": "break",
   "segments": [],
   "id": "p127"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p128.s01",
     "source": "These legends will prove of unusual interest to the general reader and especially to the scholar, thinker, and poet."
    },
    {
     "id": "p128.s02",
     "source": "They describe vividly and strongly the triumphs and the wanderings of the people of Hawaii."
    },
    {
     "id": "p128.s03",
     "source": "The legends of old Honolulu proper have been compiled from stories told by old Hawaiians still living; others, furnished by the pioneer American missionaries, who began their work on the islands early in the last century."
    },
    {
     "id": "p128.s04",
     "source": "The writer has lived among this remnant of a great race for many years, and through his sympathy and deep appreciation of native hopes and native aspirations has been able to familiarize himself with their inner life."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p128"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p129.s01",
     "source": "Price, buckram, 12mo., $1.50; also in kapa."
    },
    {
     "id": "p129.s02",
     "source": "Press of Geo."
    },
    {
     "id": "p129.s03",
     "source": "H. Ellis Co., Boston, Mass.— Overland Monthly, San Francisco, Cal., Oct."
    },
    {
     "id": "p129.s04",
     "source": "1, 1915."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p129"
  },
  {
   "type": "break",
   "segments": [],
   "id": "p130"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p131.s01",
     "source": "\"Legends of Old Honolulu\" is an interesting summary of what is known about the Hawaiian Islands, their people, and the origin of their race."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p131"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p132.s01",
     "source": "As soon as the Hawaiian alphabet was prepared, in 1821, native writers began delving into their past, finding there a treasure-mine of romantic stories and of valuable ethnological and historical facts in regard to the Polynesian race."
    },
    {
     "id": "p132.s02",
     "source": "These stories were written originally in Hawaiian, for native news-papers, and have been collected and translated by Mr. W. D. Westervelt, author of previous volumes on this same subject."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p132"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p133.s01",
     "source": "While the book will be of special interest to students of ethnology and to those who have visited Honolulu, the romantic charm which pervades this Pacific archipelago gives its history a universal attraction for the reading public."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p133"
  },
  {
   "type": "prose",
   "segments": [
    {
     "id": "p134.s01",
     "source": "The volume is well bound and well illustrated."
    },
    {
     "id": "p134.s02",
     "source": "Boston: Geo."
    },
    {
     "id": "p134.s03",
     "source": "H. Ellis Co.— Globe, Boston, Oct."
    },
    {
     "id": "p134.s04",
     "source": "25, 1915."
    }
   ],
   "id": "p134"
  },
  {
   "type": "break",
   "segments": [],
   "id": "p135"
  }
 ]
}